what is going on everybody and welcome  to the return of the python plays grand  theft auto  series  so  what's changed what is this project if  you're not familiar with the project it  was from about five years ago  back when the open ai universe was set  to include uh grand theft auto 5 which  was such a cool sandbox if you don't  know what the universe was or is sort of  i'm not really sure it kind of was  anyway it was like a collection of  environments kind of like the open ai  gym i forget what necessarily  differentiated universe from gym but a  collection of environments for you to  train and play within with your ai so  this was set to include grand theft auto  5 but then it was pulled kind of um not  i don't know about last minute but kind  of after a while it seemed like they  were pretty close to having it done and  then all of a sudden it wasn't gonna  happen so  um around this time i started to wonder  like well do i really need any of this  can we not just use a screen recorder  essentially screenshot because  constantly the frames of the game  you can simulate key presses with  virtual key presses and obviously you  can read key presses with like any type  of key logger you want so so doing all  of this in python totally possible so  that was what spawned the original  python plays grand theft auto 5.  unfortunately this was a while back i  wasn't necessarily the best programmer  in the world and i didn't really know  all that much about deep learning this  was kind of my first big deep learning  project despite all that it actually  worked okay worked surprisingly better  than it should have in my opinion at the  time and in my opinion now it wasn't the  greatest driver ever  but it definitely learned stuff in the  game and that was really cool to see the  problem was all the data was created  manually by me and anytime you made even  the slightest change in that data so  maybe you want to change the driving  style the vehicle maybe you want to take  away there was a wonder about like the  mini-map how how big of a  importance is that mini-map to the agent  for figuring out where to go and then  later i thought well why not add  waypoints to give them like a purpose  every time you make a change like that  you have to recollect all of the data  all over again and i was doing this  manually by hand uh very very tedious  we're talking like 20 plus hours of just  playing gta but you're not really  playing you're you're working in gta  to make that driver so it wasn't really  the most enjoyable experience so what's  changed between then and now it's quite  a bit like i've learned a lot to daniel  who actually we we met over the python  plays gta series originally he's been  working with me ever since together we  wrote the neural networks from scratch  book we've learned a lot about deep  learning uh the just gpus in general  have progressed quite a bit we've just  really wanted to revisit this project  for  a long time and a few months ago the  company here kamino that built this  machine reached out and was curious  about getting me to review it and it has  four a100 gpus in it uh as soon as i saw  that they they were interested in  getting a review i was like i know  exactly what we have we must do with  this uh with this uh i don't know what  to call it it's like a it's not really a  computer it's a workstation but it's  even more than a workstation i don't  like a  data science center in a little box  so anyways they've been extremely  patient with us we've had it definitely  longer than uh was intended so thanks to  camino for that i also have quite a few  other machines which have come in handy  so one of the major changes from back  then until now is daniel has created for  us essentially like our own npc that  plays grand theft auto for us and logs  all of this data and we can tell this  npc what we want like how do we want it  to drive what you know what settings do  we want waypoints do we want to show the  minimap and all that so  this is a massive update uh just for me  to not have to make the data  but also it allows us to make data  faster so we can run as many of these  npcs as we can run gta 5 instances  also  this allows us to train a model live so  we have agents that are just constantly  playing this model and then we are also  constantly training this model so what  this means for you guys that are going  to  plausibly be tuning in to watch so we'll  be streaming it just like i did before  about 24 7 on twitch uh grand theft auto  seems to have like a memory leak or  something and it's it if you leave it up  long enough it is going to crash like  within 24 hours sometimes less sometimes  more so  close to 24 7 we'll we'll be trying to  stream this and unlike before when i  would train a model basically i had to  train a model and then update the model  that the agent actually playing the game  and streaming the game to twitch that  was a static model that model did not  change  unless i  you know unless i manually changed that  model basically it wasn't learning live  as it played the game in this case uh  the play the agent that is playing is  playing on the most recent up-to-date  model whatever it is that we're training  and it is constantly being updated and  this has been really really fun just as  we've been trying to prepare and get  everything ready for the stream we've  been training different models and and  just seeing them grow and change like in  real time is really cool i mean  obviously you see like your loss curve  go down or something but very rarely do  you get to see like a constant  representation of where is your model  right now uh and it's been really fun to  watch so hopefully if you tune into the  stream you should be able to tune in  especially if it's a newish model you  should be able to tune in like a few  hours later and it should be a little  different and then as it matures it  might take days or something to to get  actually much better from the previous  point but our intention here is to spend  a little bit more time streaming like  the model's growth over time and the  changes and all that and then also we'd  like to be showing things like  visualizations of the neural network so  like the training and all that as it's  happening live as well as things like  visualizing the convolutional layers uh  and so on so um i wouldn't come to the  stream expecting to see like a really  good self-driving car uh self-driving  cars is almost like a general  intelligence problem rather than just a  simple supervised learning task or  something like that i mean we billion  dollar companies haven't solved it now  we have a trillion dollar company that  still hasn't solved it self-driving cars  were uh one to three years away five  years ago and i would say i'm pretty  sure we're still saying they're one to  three years away today so it's a very  hard challenge but it's very fun and uh  the the actual like growth is is pretty  linear so even if you don't have a  perfect driver uh you can still see that  oh it's actually making attempts to do  cool things so uh and it's at least okay  it just might crash once in a while  which is fine in grand theft auto it's  just not quite fine for real life so uh  we have it a little easier we can have a  little more fun with it and all that so  anyways  we at the time of my release of this we  will be live on twitch.tv slash centex  so come check it out uh watch the model  grow we'll that isn't going to be like  the only model that we train we're going  to try to tweak things um add different  concepts to it right now it's mostly  visual we don't really have any other  sensor data that's coming in we could  add things like speedometer or proximity  sensors that kind of stuff also like  historical data right now it's just  frame by frame so we have lots of ideas  that we want to test but i really just  wanted to get it out get the stream out  because each time we test our ideas like  i said even as we've been building it uh  it's really cool to like watch the model  grow and kind of see what are the  differences between these models uh and  all that so anyways definitely come  check out the stream if you're  interested in this sort of thing it's  just it's just cool to watch it watch it  grow and change over time uh and then  also if you have any ideas suggestions  all that we'll  certainly be open to input from people  so  anyway that's it come check us out on  twitch.tv slash syntax otherwise i will  see you guys in another video  or on the stream

